{{Nihongo| '''PYRAMID30 Inc.'''|ピラミッド30株式会社|Kabushiki-gaisha Piramiddo-Sāti}}, established in 2017, is the [[holding company]] at the head of a Sanese [[corporate group]] that supplies [[statistics]] and information on music and the [[Music industry of Sane|music industry in Sane]] and the greater [[Adonia]]n music scene. It became widely known across the continent for its definitive [[music chart]]s.<ref name="smprs"/> 

The charts are compiled from data drawn from over 40,000 retail and digital outlets across the nations and provide sales rankings of music CDs, digital downloads, streaming metrics, and other entertainment products based on weekly tabulations. Results are announced every Tuesday and published by subsidiary PYRAMID30 Entertainment Inc. 

PYRAMID30 started publishing its Combined Chart, which includes physical sales, digital sales, and streaming together, seamlessly integrating modern listening habits into their historic tracking model.

==History==
{{Expand section|date=January 2020}}
[[File:PYRAMID30 Logo 2017.svg|thumb|left|upright|Logo from 2017 to present]]
PYRAMID30 Inc. was founded in June 2017 by a coalition of former broadcasting executives and independent music promoters in Okami. Their primary goal was to create a modernized, completely transparent tracking system for music consumption that could adapt to the rapid shift from physical media to digital streaming. Before PYRAMID30, chart data in the region was notoriously fragmented, relying heavily on localized physical record shop sales while largely ignoring growing online communities. To bridge this gap, the company began publishing a combined digital and physical singles chart on an experimental basis in the summer of 2017. 

Early on, PYRAMID30 functioned solely as an exclusive, subscription-based information resource. Its primary clientele consisted of industry executives, talent agencies, and A&R representatives who used the raw data to scout emerging trends in the underground hip-hop and mainstream pop scenes. However, to establish the chart as the definitive industry standard, the company launched an intensive public promotional campaign. By integrating their weekly top 30 countdowns with national broadcasting networks and late-night music television programs across Okaiken, the hit parade quickly transitioned from an insider industry tool to a household name. 

As the platform's cultural influence grew, the company expanded its data collection infrastructure to include neighboring music markets. By 2019, PYRAMID30 had established dedicated tracking branches in both Nakamizu and Yanbaru, standardizing music metrics across the three nations. This regional integration allowed for a much more comprehensive and accurate understanding of the continental music landscape.

Today, the company also releases syndicated publications and digital magazines across Adonia dedicated to broader entertainment media. These publications focus heavily on tracking the international success and cultural export of artists from Okaiken, Nakamizu, and Yanbaru, serving as a vital bridge between domestic talent and the global Adonian audience.

==Policy==
PYRAMID30 monitors and reports on sales of CDs, digital tracks, streaming numbers, and entertainment content in several other formats. Charts are published every Tuesday on PYRAMID30's official network. Every Monday, PYRAMID30 receives data from outlets, though independent merchandise sold through grassroots channels is occasionally excluded if the vendor is not registered within the tracking network. Before data was collected entirely via automated digital streams, the charts were compiled using aggregate data drops from major record chains.

== Controversy ==
In 2020, PYRAMID30 faced a dispute when an independent music journalist suggested that the organization was manipulating its statistics to benefit certain legacy management companies. The journalist questioned the validity of PYRAMID30's hit chart on the grounds that its streaming aggregation methods for international Adonian plays were not entirely transparent. Several independent media organizations advocated for the journalist's right to free expression. A local District Court initially reviewed the case, but PYRAMID30 ultimately opted for a private settlement to improve their algorithm's transparency, leading to the creation of the more accurate Combined Chart system. No criminal charges were laid, and the methodology was subsequently made public.

